#
# Two printf answers that are zsh's alone.
#
# A format beginning with a dash is a format in zsh and an option error in
# bash, ksh93 and dash. `printf '-> %s\n' x` is a working line here and a
# usage error in the other three, which is why the portable spelling of it
# needs the `--` the core file asks for.
#
# And a numeric operand that is a number with something after it: bash, ksh93
# and dash all convert the part they could read and write 12, where zsh reads
# the operand as a math expression, fails the whole of it and writes 0. The
# status is 1 in all four, so a script testing the status sees no difference
# and a script reading the value sees a different number.

printf '-> [%s]\n' 'a leading dash is not an option here' 2> /dev/null
printf 'that was %s\n' "$?"

printf '[%d]\n' 12x 2> /dev/null
printf 'that was %s\n' "$?"
printf '[%d]\n' 34junk 2> /dev/null

# A well-formed operand converts the same way it does everywhere, which is
# what makes the line above about the trailing text rather than about %d.
printf '[%d][%d]\n' 12 -34
